5 #edb/ref 17592186442597 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442605 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442605 1077 "selection" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442605 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442580 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442605 1079 #uuid "9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442583 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442606 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442606 1077 "field-1" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442606 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442583 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442606 1079 #uuid "9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442566 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442579 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442596 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442598 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442599 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1077 "selection" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442596 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442607 1079 #uuid "be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442607 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1069 "q-127596" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,1,1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1072 #uuid "35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442608 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{({e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}(-⟨0,1,1⟩+2⟨0,1,0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0(0)+1(1)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442601 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1069 "q-223120" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨\\,0\\,,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1072 #uuid "7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442609 1074 "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-2|\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442605 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1069 "q-74850" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1071 "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩,\\,t\\ge 0 \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1072 #uuid "7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442610 1074 "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩+({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442603 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442611 1069 "q-127597" 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534884 17592186045417 true] [17592186442611 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{al